  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Sat Apr 18 2026\n\nCold front is now south of the CWA with some decent north winds \nbehind it. Continued pressure rises just east of our area should \nallow winds to turn more east throughout today. Strongest winds \neast/southeast starting early tonight into Sunday morning before \ndiminishing some later Sunday. Expect winds of 15-25 mph with \ngusts to 30 mph during this overnight period, and gusts as high as\n40 mph possible along west slopes of mountains. \n\nThis surge in southeast winds will bring some low level moisture \ninto the area Sunday. Aloft, a weak disturbance just west of the \nBaja, will move up to the CWA later Sunday. These two features \nwill allow for a slight chance of showers Sunday afternoon/night. \nMoisture axis will slowly lift northeast with the upper \ndisturbance Monday. This will keep a chance of showers going, \nthough mostly limited to the mountains of the north. \n\nTuesday through Saturday...short wave ridge moves in Tuesday \nbehind the exiting upper disturbance. This will flush moisture out\nof the CWA and clear the skies. Next Pacific system moves to \nNoCal Tuesday, Great Basin Wednesday, and then crosses the central\nRockies Thursday. This ushers in a stretch of dry, warm weather \ninto the weekend, with winds gradually increasing each day. Breezy\non Wednesday with west winds of 15-25 mph, increasing Thursday to\nwest 20-30 mph with higher gusts. Winds diminishing some Friday \nand Saturday but still breezy in the afternoons. Fire conditions \nwill slowly worsen through the week.",
